// The deliverable, checked here rather than in the developer's project.
//
// `templates/` is the game Create Kei MMO exists to emit; `src/` is only the
// machinery that copies it. So this is the config that guards the product, and
// `tsconfig.json` next to it guards the machinery.
//
// It extends `templates/tsconfig.json` — the config that ships *inside* a
// generated project — rather than restating its options, so the two cannot
// drift. A developer who runs `bun run typecheck` in their new game runs these
// compiler options, and changing theirs changes this.
//
// Only `include` differs. The paths in theirs are relative to a project root
// that does not exist yet, and the three placeholder identifiers a template
// still carries need declaring before any of it parses as TypeScript.
//
// What the templates import is resolved out of this package's own
// `devDependencies`, at the ranges `templates/package.json` asks for. For
// `kei-transaction` that is not a coincidence to maintain by hand: `src/index.ts`
// writes this package's devDependency range into the generated manifest, so the
// SDK checked here is by construction the SDK the developer installs.
{
  "extends": "./templates/tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["templates/src", "templates/server", "templates/shared", "types/template-placeholders.d.ts"]
}
